#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kiwi-server-gui — see a fleet, edit its file, build its install media.

The window is a fleet on the left (the overview, one row per host, the defaults)
and on the right what is known about the selection: the machines' own answers
(`kiwi-server status --porcelain`, asked every minute while the window has the
focus) and the host's settings. The form comes from two things: the base fields
every host has (target, role, admin, network, disk, updates) and the settings
schema the selected role publishes through `kiwi-server roles --porcelain` — so a
new role dropped into roles/ gets its form without any change here.

Everything that generates or builds goes through the CLI (`kiwi-server
render` / `build`), streamed into the log pane. The GUI edits YAML and reads
the CLI's --porcelain JSON; it is not a second implementation of anything, and
what counts as a problem on a machine is decided there too.
Comments in a fleet file are not kept when the GUI saves it.
"""
